# GridSignals in-container scheduler (R3.1). The Dockerfile installs this file to
# /etc/cron.d/gridsignals; deploy/entrypoint.sh starts the cron daemon before it
# execs uvicorn. Debian /etc/cron.d format: m h dom mon dow USER command.
# Times are UTC (the image sets no timezone), matching R10.2.
#
# Four rules this file must keep, each pinned in tests/test_packaging.py because
# each one fails SILENTLY:
#
# 1. Data refresh drives deploy/ingest_pipeline.sh — the ONE canonical pipeline,
#    shared with the first-load entrypoint. A second hardcoded step list here
#    would be exactly the drift those tests exist to prevent. (The reporting
#    jobs below are not pipeline steps: they read what the pipeline produced and
#    write nothing, so they are scheduled on their own requirement's cadence.)
# 2. Every job sources /etc/gridsignals.env first, and treats a failure to do so
#    as fatal. cron strips the environment, so GRIDSIGNALS_DB (a Dockerfile ENV)
#    and any optional API key are invisible to a scheduled run otherwise — which
#    would turn a deliberate skip-on-missing-key into a source that silently
#    never runs while the ledger reads healthy. The snapshot is written at
#    startup, mode 600, outside the repo tree: NO secret is ever assigned in this
#    file (R10.8).
# 3. Every job that WRITES the store is wrapped in deploy/scheduled_run.sh,
#    which serializes scheduled ticks against each other and records a dated
#    skip (exit 0) when a manual or first-load ingest already holds the per-step
#    ingestion lock (R3.2) — that lock raises, it does not skip. Read the
#    guard's header for what this does NOT cover: the per-step lock is not held
#    for the tick, so a manual run started mid-tick can still interleave.
#    The two reporting jobs below are deliberately NOT wrapped. They take no
#    lock and write nothing, so they serialize against nothing — and the guard
#    exits 0 on contention, which would DISCARD the record instead of delaying
#    it. deploy/entrypoint.sh backgrounds a first-load ingest, so a container
#    restart near a report's slot would silently lose one month of R9.3 and
#    THREE months of R10.7 while cron logged success.
# 4. Each job is a brace group with the redirect OUTSIDE it, so a failure of the
#    env source or the `cd` — the two likeliest tick-killers — reaches the log
#    instead of cron's mail channel, which has no MTA in this image. Same reason
#    for MAILTO="".
#
# NOT scheduled here: the annual entity-identifier refresh (R4.2).
# app/enrich_entities.py writes reviewable seed CSVs to disk and never writes the
# store — generated fills are a reviewed-in-PR artifact by design. In a container
# those CSVs land on ephemeral storage and are never loaded, so a cron line here
# would only pretend R4.2 was automated. It stays an operator-run refresh
# (generate -> review -> python -m app.db.load_seeds).
SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=""

# Daily full refresh: licensing -> ingest -> classify -> score -> plays -> digest.
# This is what closes "a container with a durable volume never re-ingests": the
# first-load gate deliberately skips ingest when the volume already holds
# signals, so without a schedule the dataset is frozen at first boot.
17 3 * * * root { . /etc/gridsignals.env && cd /app && sh deploy/scheduled_run.sh sh deploy/ingest_pipeline.sh ; } >> /var/log/gridsignals-cron.log 2>&1

# Monthly precision report (R9.3: "Monthly job computes precision per trigger
# type, source, and signal_scope"), 1st of the month. It writes nothing to the
# store — the record IS these log lines, dated by the run, which is what makes
# precision observable over time rather than only in the moment someone opens
# the Precision page. It reports the month that JUST ENDED (see
# app/audit/precision.py:prior_month_end): firing on the 1st, a report about
# "this month" would summarize a few hours and be false forever.
#
# Unguarded on purpose (rule 3 above): read-only, lock-free, and a guard skip
# would throw the month's record away rather than defer it. Mid-afternoon UTC,
# ~12h from the 03:17 refresh, so it reads a settled store.
47 15 1 * * root { . /etc/gridsignals.env && cd /app && python -m app.audit.precision --report ; } >> /var/log/gridsignals-cron.log 2>&1

# Quarterly license re-verification sweep. R10.7 itself names a "semi-annual
# task"; this job runs more often than that literal minimum so the sweep stays
# comfortably inside stale_facts's 180-day window with margin — a 181-day gap
# (Jan 1 / Jul 1) let a fact re-verified 2027-01-20 read due=0 on 2027-07-01 at
# only 162 days old, and go unnamed until 2028-01-01 at age_days=346. Jan/Apr/Jul/
# Oct 1st gives ~90-91 day gaps regardless of month-length variance; same
# mid-afternoon reasoning as above, 36 minutes clear of it. This is the JOB half
# only: it names the facts whose verified_date has aged past the window so the
# work announces itself instead of waiting for someone to open Admin.
# Re-verifying a fact against primary Microsoft sources stays a human act; the
# sweep writes nothing and never invents a verified_date. Unguarded for the same
# reason as the report above, and more sharply: a discarded tick here costs three
# months, not one.
23 16 1 1,4,7,10 * root { . /etc/gridsignals.env && cd /app && python -m app.reverify ; } >> /var/log/gridsignals-cron.log 2>&1
